188:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
195:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
199:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the form is now `borrow [mut] view = source [from a until b] { … } [else { … }]`; the `as view` spelling above is superseded.*
200:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the example's unlabeled pattern (`S { a }`) and unannotated local are superseded by items 9 and 18; the typed replacement comes in the spelling round (R-0625).*
213:- **Q1.7** Where does release information live? — Stable release state and raw context; no safe references. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q6): release-context erasure is allowed only with a proof (release statically known, no per-instance information), including owning Text where applicable; no unconditional one-pointer claim.*
234:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the address is read from a read-only target profile; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile is unchanged, and the provenance rules and the zero-byte exception are preserved.*
236:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): over the target profile's address spaces; the reconstruction policy is unchanged.*
237:- **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the existing profile keeps the `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound; any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound.*
245:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): subviews use `borrow [mut] s = t from a until b { … } [else { … }]`; the `as s` spelling is superseded.*
246:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the form is `borrow [mut] view = source [from a until b] { ... } [else { ... }]`.*
252:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `as` is removed; the named lossless conversion takes its place in the table.*
254:- **Q6.4** Does unreachable code receive static refusal checks? — Check all written code, including generic bodies. *Revises or adds: REVISES TYPE-22@1 examples.*
258:- **Q7.1** How are target grant schemas and multiplicities specified? — One target, linux-x86_64-hosted (Linux ≥ 5.6), with a fixed grant table; main asks for grants as parameters. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): the grant table is a lawful extensible registry rather than a permanently closed one.*
267:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.* *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): the grant table is a lawful extensible registry; the existing grant contracts are preserved, an unknown operation is refused, and the Entropy grant's shape waits for slice 6 ([question 14](#foundation-q14)).*
280:- **Q9.5** Are heap opening and borrowing separate user mechanisms? — One surface borrow; distinct typed Core cases. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the one surface borrow is written `borrow [mut] view = source ...`.*
282:- **Q9.7** How are primitive calls specified? — One exhaustive typed primitive-contract inventory. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q16): lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; exhaustive coverage of the selected registry; an unknown operation is refused.*
302:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-p5"></a>**P5** legacy/ is a byte-frozen snapshot, never patched or run — legacy/ is main's tree at the move commit, byte-frozen. A root check compares its tree hash, allowing only deletions a satisfied RETENTION.tsv entry authorizes. No legacy gate ever runs. The oracle is the tag-built artifact (P13), not the legacy/ copy. *Revised 2026-11-08 (owner, "ok to 1"): legacy/ also grows by byte-for-byte sweeps recorded in scripts/checks/legacy.lock; no file, once frozen or swept, is ever rewritten ([sweep row](#decided-2026-11-08-legacy-sweep)).*
305:- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task. *Revised 2026-11-08 (owner, "yes redesign"): the landing bot is removed; the landing services are the train, its review leg, the hook and the gates ([landing redesign](#decided-2026-11-08-landing-redesign)).*
319:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.* *Revised 2026-11-08 (owner, "ok to 2"): all of docs/archive/ moves under legacy/, these retained records included, so docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ is no longer part of the day-one root; docs/decisions.md stays the one home of decisions and their quotes, and git history keeps the originals ([archive move](#decided-2026-11-08-archive-move)).*
321:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
