1568:| OD6 | <a id="od6"></a>Traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity | D7; implemented by bug 186's repair (TRAIT-1 – TRAIT-9) | bug 186's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0548, R-0549; V5 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity. Completed 2026-11-02: qualified as `Trait::method(&x, ...)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current qualification rule: this OD6 as completed by the audit below; the audit supplies syntax, not a second rule.** **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no method calls: a trait call is `Trait::f(x)`; coherence stands.** |
1573:| OD11 | <a id="od11"></a>Casts: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function | T-CAST: ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8 (2026-10-21) and §4.5's value-domain rule; not implemented | exit 2k; the V1 increment that admits casts | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `as` is removed; the value-domain rule moves to one named lossless conversion.** |
1585:| OD23 | <a id="od23"></a>REF-11: every argument of a call, the receiver included, is overlap-checked against its root, re-lent `&mut` bindings included (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | the current compiler exempts a re-lent reference binding (REF-11@2), so `r.add(r.step)` is accepted; bug 319 | exit 2q; R-0499 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): every argument of a call, the receiver included, is overlap-checked against the root, including re-lent `&mut` bindings, so `r.add(r.step)` is refused; read the Copy field into a `let` first. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319) **Canonical current rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this is the historical clarification of its re-lent case.** **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there are no receivers or dot calls; every argument is overlap-checked, so `Type::add(r, r.step)` is refused.** |
1592:| OD30 | <a id="od30"></a>MEM-4: `e.f` requires the struct or a reference to it (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | a field access opens one `Heap` layer implicitly; bug 326 | exit 2q; R-0499 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): `e.f` requires `e` to be the struct or a reference to it; opening a `Heap` or `HeapArray` uses an explicit non-consuming place form; `*` stays the consuming move-out. Now MEM-4@3 (pending, bug 326); the place form's spelling is OD36 **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the consuming move-out is the named move-out operation, not `*`.** |
1594:| OD32 | <a id="od32"></a>OWN-15: no receiver auto-borrow (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | a `&self` or `&mut self` method on an owned place borrows it implicitly; bug 328 | exit 2q; R-0499 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): no receiver auto-borrow: a method with `&self` or `&mut self` on an owned place is written `(&s).m()` or `(&mut s).m()`; a receiver that is already a reference is unchanged. Now OWN-15@3 (pending, bug 328) **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no receivers and no method calls, so `(&s).m()` is gone.** |
1598:| OD36 | <a id="od36"></a>The spelling of the explicit non-consuming place form that opens a `Heap` or `HeapArray` (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD30, which requires one and keeps `*` as the consuming move-out). Options: a borrow through the cell (`(&h).f`, `(&mut h).f`), a named projection (`h.open().f`), or a dedicated operator. Recommendation: the borrow form, which needs no new syntax and matches OD32's explicit receiver borrow Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only through a borrow block (`borrow h as r { r.f }`), no `(&h).f`; specify the result type for `Heap<T>`, `&Heap<T>` and `&mut Heap<T>`, one heap layer per block (MEM-4), and `HeapArray` elements | the current compiler opens one heap layer implicitly (`h.f` on `Heap<S>`, bug 326) | bug 326's repair (MEM-4) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only through a borrow block; no `(&h).f` |
1601:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): generated interfaces keep bodyless declarations; handwritten ordinary bodyless functions are refused.** |
1604:| OD42 | <a id="od42"></a>Comparisons on `bool` and `char` under OD26 (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26, whose answer says "arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only"). Two readings: numbers only, so `b == true` and `c < 'z'` are refused (a `bool` is tested directly, a `char` converted by a named function); or "numbers" excludes pointers only, and `bool` and `char` keep equality (and `char` ordering). Recommendation: keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, and refuse only pointers, which is what OD26's alignment with TRUST-1 is about Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, refuse ordering on `bool`; amend TYPE-6 and OD26 explicitly | the current compiler compares `bool` and `char` | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `==`/`!=` on bool/char and `<` on char; refuse ordering on bool **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there is no `char`; `==` and `!=` stay on `bool`, and character comparisons become explicit `u8` and UTF-8 operations.** |
1619:| OD57 | <a id="od57"></a>The region name of a borrow block (added 2026-10-30, item S12b of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a borrow block keeps its region name (`borrow x as r in R { }`) or drops `in R`. Options: (a) keep `in R`; (b) drop it, the block itself being the region. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) drop `in R`. Austral: the borrow statement names its region (`borrow x as r in R do ... end`); anonymous borrows `&x` in call arguments name none | the region name is written and has no other use in the language | REF-8, REF-11, SCOPE-D1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Drop `in R` |
1621:| OD59 | <a id="od59"></a>One way to consume a `Heap` (added 2026-10-30, item S7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Which operations consume a `Heap<T>`: the move-out `*h` (OD30), `destroy(h)`, `free(h)` or a new `take(h)`. Options: (a) keep `*h` and `free(h)` as today; (b) `*h` as move-out-and-free and `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted, no `take`; (c) `take(h)` replaces `*h` as the move-out, `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b): `*h` and a `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, no `free`, no `take` (a `take` would be another spelling of `*h`). Austral: not checked | `*h` moves out and frees and `free(h)` frees the cell and returns its value (MEM-5); `destroy(h)` on a Heap is refused (E0272: destroy() requires a named type) | MEM-5, OWN-13, OPEN-36 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `*h` + `destroy(h)`, no `free`, no `take` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): a named move-out replaces `*h`; `destroy(h)` stays, no `free`, no `take`.** |
1622:| OD60 | <a id="od60"></a>One consuming receiver form for `Destroy` (added 2026-10-30, item S8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Destroy` is implemented through one consuming receiver form only. Options: (a) keep the receiver forms the current compiler accepts; (b) one consuming form (`fn destroy(self)`) only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) one consuming form. Austral: no destructor trait; a linear type's module exports an ordinary consuming function | a `Destroy` impl may take `self` or `&self`; `&mut self` is refused (E0587) | OWN-13, TRAIT-9, TRAIT-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One consuming form **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the one consuming `Destroy` form stays, written as a typed first parameter.** |
1624:| OD62 | <a id="od62"></a>Unused values and variables (added 2026-10-30, item Q3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an unused local variable or parameter is refused (Go's rule) and whether an unused `Result` or `Option` may be dropped by `discard`. Options: (a) refuse only an unused `Result` or `Option` (OWN-6); (b) Go's rule: any unused local or parameter is refused (an intentionally unused parameter is named `_`), and `discard` refuses `Result` and `Option`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b), stated as one invariant: every name a body binds is used or is `_` (a bodyless declaration has no body, so needs no exception); `discard` and wildcard drops refuse `Result` and `Option`, Copy or not; to clarify: `_` as a loop index, and an unused `&mut` binding under REF-10. Austral: not checked | unused Copy locals and parameters are accepted; OWN-6 refuses an unused fallible result | OWN-5, OWN-6, OWN-7, REF-10 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Every bound name used or `_`; `discard` refuses Result/Option **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): a dead store to a `mut` local is also refused.** |
1639:| OD77 | <a id="od77"></a>Resync the grammar to the decided language (added 2026-10-30, item B of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `grammar/concrete.ebnf` is resynchronized for the items the language already decided: `E#V` becomes `E::V`, binding renaming, a function body with no trailing value and `;` for bodyless functions, no generic trait methods, assignment targets are places, unknown attribute names refused, and no `break <expr>`. Options: (a) approve the resync, as actual productions with a factored `place` prefix for assignment; (b) defer it, the divergences staying recorded in grammar/conformance_known.tsv (the decided language does not change either way). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) approve, with actual productions, a `place` prefix and `break <expr>` removed. Austral: not applicable | the grammar is stale on these items (grammar/conformance_known.tsv) | SYN-1, PAT-1, STMT-6, STMT-8, OWN-8, FFI-6 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Approve resync with actual productions **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the resync refuses handwritten ordinary bodyless functions.** |
1642:| OD80 | <a id="od80"></a>`assume` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assume expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `assume`, tracked: every claim depending on it is marked assumed **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `assume` cannot admit an operation in safe code.** **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `assume` is removed only after its replacement is shown on real examples; until then it stays explicit and tracked.** |
1646:| OD84 | <a id="od84"></a>Struct destructuring `let` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains struct destructuring `let S { a } = e;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: destructuring `let` of records exists (not checked) | the parser accepts it (PAT-5) | PAT-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add struct-destructuring `let` |
1652:| OD90 | <a id="od90"></a>`struct Copy <T>` and contextual keywords (added 2026-10-30, item Copy of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Copy` stays a contextual keyword, with the LL(1) checkers taught to model it, or becomes a reserved word (the other unreserved words, `self`, `repr`, `packed` and `align`, stay as they are unless decided separately). Options: (a) reserve `Copy` as a keyword, refusing it as an identifier; (b) keep it contextual, the LL(1) checkers taught to model a quoted unreserved terminal as also IDENT, each conflict found listed for the owner (letters as in the divergence report). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) reserve `Copy`: one lexical rule, consistent with OD50 (reserved `from` and `until`) and OD43 (builtin names refused as user names). Austral: its keywords are reserved | contextual; the `struct Copy <T>` conflict is invisible to the checkers' token model | SYN-1, OWN-D1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserve `Copy` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `self` is an ordinary identifier with no special meaning; `Copy` stays reserved.** |
1653:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): `as` is removed, so the question no longer arises.** |
1660:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
1673:| OD111 | <a id="od111"></a>Misaligned fields of a packed struct (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a `#[repr(packed)]` struct may hold a field whose alignment exceeds the alignment of its offset, and whether such a field may be borrowed. Bears on FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5. The current compiler: both accepted: `#[repr(packed)] struct P { v: u8, l: u32 }` places `l` at offset 1, and `&p.l` is accepted and reads the field correctly in the binary and in `--interp`. [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) §2 restricts a packed struct to fields whose alignment does not exceed their position, without saying what enforces it. Options: (a) refuse such a field; (b) accept it and refuse a reference to it; (c) accept both, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted on both consumers | FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept field, refuse a reference to it **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no packed structs until a demonstrated packed foreign ABI reopens them.** |
1688:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
1692:| OD130 | <a id="od130"></a>Unlabeled pattern binders: by position or by name (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an unlabeled binder in a struct or variant pattern (`E::V { y, x }`) binds the field of its own name or the field at its position. Bears on PAT-1, PAT-3 and PAT-10. The current compiler disagrees with itself: with `enum E { V { x: Int, y: Int } }` and `E::V { x: 40, y: 2 }`, the arm `E::V { y, x } => y` gives 40 in `match` and in `if let` (binders zip the variant's fields in declaration order, `Concrete/Elab/Elab.lean` `bindings.zip ev.fields`), while the struct destructuring `let S { y, x } = s; y` gives 2 (by name); both consumers agree. PAT-1's `field: binder` form and the grammar's `binding_list` comment ("positional") point different ways. Options: (a) by name (field punning) everywhere; (b) by position everywhere; (c) refuse an unlabeled list whose order differs from the declaration. Austral: not applicable (no punning). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the variant-pattern disagreement may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-130 | see OPEN-130 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): By name everywhere **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): every pattern writes `field: binder`.** |
1695:| OD133 | <a id="od133"></a>User functions named `print`, `println` or `append` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a program may declare a function named `print`, `println` or `append`, which then replaces the builtin of that name in calls the program writes. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-8 and TYPE-10. The current compiler: accepted; with `fn print(x: Int) -> Int`, `print(42)` calls it (exit 42 on both consumers), because Elab desugars the variadic builtins only when no function of that name is visible, and MOD-3's reserved names do not include them. MOD-8 already binds the calls the compiler itself generates by identity. Options: (a) reserve the three names (MOD-3); (b) allow the replacement, as the current compiler does. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-133 | see OPEN-133 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reserve the three names **Revised 2026-11-08 by [language decision item 17](#decided-2026-11-08-language): the builtin output and append forms are removed, superseding the reservation of `print`, `println` and `append`. The naming policy for ordinary output and append functions stays open to the [spelling round](#decided-2026-11-08-spelling-round).** |
1699:| OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there is no `char` type or character literal.** |
1709:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): no `repr(C, packed)` until a demonstrated packed foreign ABI reopens it.** |
1713:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): multi-level paths are written in import declarations; an imported item is used qualified by its module's last segment (`b::f` for `a::b::f`).** |
