  11. <a id="decided-2026-11-08-f7"></a>*The termination wording* (ISS-0014 F7): "every function has
      termination evidence with its premises, or a report naming the explicitly permitted
      unresolved obligations". It replaces "termination is known beforehand", which overstated what a
      d-site permission establishes, in the [no-hidden-traps row](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps),
      README.md, PHILOSOPHY.md and the pending no-hidden-traps changelog fragment.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-std-table"></a>**A std-per-slice table, no user-guide chapters for now, the written grammar and the speed target**
  (owner, 2026-11-08: "ok let's do 2, for now let's not do 3"). Item 2 is approved: `ROADMAP.md`
  gets a table of which standard-library module arrives in which slice, with its spec contract, its
  std source, its proof status and the showcases that need it
  ([table](../ROADMAP.md#std-per-slice)). Item 3, a user-guide chapter per slice, is not done for
  now; it has no roadmap item. Item 1 was then approved (owner, 2026-11-08: "ok do the written
  grammar"): <a id="decided-2026-11-08-written-grammar"></a>before the parser is built, the full
  surface grammar is written down for the owner's review, every construct with its decided row and
  the open spellings flagged; after approval it is encoded in Lean as the independent grammar
  judgment (row Q8.3), and only then is the parser built against it (R-0622). Item 4 was approved
  too (owner, 2026-11-08: "and yes speed target is fine"):
  <a id="decided-2026-11-08-speed-target"></a>speed and size stay report-only until the first real
  showcase (grep, slice 16); then a performance target against C is set from measured data, as an
  owner decision (R-0623).
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-capabilities-kept"></a>**Capabilities stay as decided: `with(...)` permission
  sets plus grant values** (owner, 2026-11-08: "i don't see an isue with noisier if it's clearer").
  This confirms the existing design with no revision: the [authority rules](#decided-2026-11-02-authority)
  and the Q5 and Q7 rows stand as written, and foundation contract C5 is written to them. The
  answer closes a comparison with a capabilities-as-values-only (object-capability) model, in
  which all four reviewing models recommended keeping today's design, because `with(...)` is the
  checked ceiling in a function's signature when structs own capabilities. Follow-up for the
  allocation slice: whether destroying a collection still needs `with(Alloc)` when its release
  metadata travels with it (R-0624, raised by agy).
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-language"></a>**Language and process decisions of 2026-11-08** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "record, yes to all recommended", approving the list as recommended; "i prefer fully
  gone, self and as are irrelevant from what i see", on `self` and `as`; and "i like 3", on how
  imported items are used). Codex, Kimi and agy reviewed the list in six rounds and agreed on every
        trailing comma is optional.
    44. *Imports (OPEN-11):* `import a::b::{x, y};` with a nonempty item list. There is no renaming
        and no module import, which all three reviewers rejected as a second form against the MOD-2
        manifest. Uses follow 11(b).
    45. *Spec functions (OPEN-13):* `spec fn f(...) -> T = e;` and `assert(e) by path(args);`. Spec
        functions use a pure subset, and the name restriction on `ghost let` is fixed.
    46. *Generic parameters (OPEN-14):* type parameters come before capability parameters (`cap C`).
        Unions use `+`, and `{}` is empty. Capability names are never ordinary types.
    47. *Blocks (OPEN-16):* the decided statement and value block behaviour is kept, with the
        common-prefix LL(1) grammar proved.
    48. *Match (OPEN-17):* enum patterns are qualified, and every arm ends with a comma.
    49. *Labels (OPEN-18):* both loop forms take labels (`'label: while`, `'label: for`), with
        `break 'label;` and `continue 'label;`. There are no empty statements.
    50. *Arrays (OPEN-20):* no empty array literals; empty structs are kept.
    51. *Brands (OPEN-22):* an explicit `exists`/`pack`/`unpack` brand surface, and named binder
        contracts on function types.
    52. *Kept arguments (OPEN-23):* `kept buf: H`, with its paired `Kept<H>` completion, and no
        borrow-derived kept arguments (Q3.12 in full). Only pointer qualification is left.
    53. *Ratified (OPEN-25):* value `while ... else` and postfix member `->` stay refused.
  - *Later, with no decision now.*
    54. The Core freeze (A2c), after the spec-core proofs and A2i integration.
    55. <a id="decided-2026-11-08-spelling-round"></a>*The spelling round:* the exact names and
        signatures of the lossless conversion, the move-out, the mathematical-arithmetic boundary,
        output and append, and the typing rules for destructuring and ghost bindings, together
        with every spelling from items 6–29 and 40–53. They are shown to the owner as before/after code.
    56. The entropy grant's shape, at slice 6.
    57. The concurrency model, at slice 28.
    58. Niches and smaller enum tags, only if a workload needs them.
    59. The remaining open grammar sub-rules come back to the owner in the spelling round with
        item 55, as before/after code:
        - the numeric token policy (OPEN-5);
        - attribute duplication, ordering, attachment and scope, the struct-invariant scope
          included (OPEN-15);
        - zero-count array repetition (OPEN-20);
        - the closed builtin inventory, and exhaustive signatures for the view, constant-time,
          text, container and branded APIs (OPEN-4/21);
        - trait and inherent impl shells (OPEN-12);
        - brand binder scope, escape and loop-carried existential rules (OPEN-22).
  - <a id="decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions"></a>*Rows this revises*, each an explicit owner
    revision of the row named:
    - [OD6](#od6), [OD32](#od32), [OD60](#od60) (items 6, 7): there are no method calls and no
      receivers. A trait call is `Trait::f(x)`; an ambiguous call is still qualified, and
      [OD6](#od6)'s coherence stands. OD32's `(&s).m()` form is gone. OD60's single consuming
      `Destroy` form stays, written with a typed first parameter.
    - [OD130](#od130) (item 9): every pattern writes `field: binder`.
    - [OD23](#od23) (items 6, 7): the overlap rule applies to ordinary arguments; there is no receiver.
    - [OD11](#od11), [OD91](#od91), Q6.2 (item 11a): `as` is gone. OD11's value-domain rule moves to the
      named lossless conversion, and OD91's cast-precedence question disappears.
    - [OD30](#od30), the [allocation rules](#decided-2026-11-02-allocation) (item 12): the consuming
      move-out is the named operation.
    - [OD59](#od59) (item 12): the move-out is a named operation, not `*h`. `destroy(h)` stays, and
      there is still no `free` or `take`.
    - TYPE-20, [OD137](#od137) and [OD42](#od42) (item 16): there is no `char` type and no character
      literal; only `bool` keeps `==` and `!=` from OD42.
    - Q9.8 (item 21): kept as decided, confirmed with no change.
    - [OD39](#od39), [OD77](#od77) (item 22): a bodyless declaration appears only in generated
      interfaces, trait signatures and `extern`, and the grammar resync refuses it elsewhere.
    - [OD111](#od111), [OD147](#od147) (item 23): no `packed` and no `repr(C, packed)` until a
      demonstrated packed foreign ABI reopens them.
    - [OD80](#od80) (item 24): `assume` stays explicit and tracked until its replacement is shown,
      and is then removed.
    - Q6.4, TYPE-22 (item 26): compile-time `Copy` constants. The rows are revised once the
      closed initializer subset is defined and the reopening conditions are checked.
    - [OD62](#od62) (item 29): a dead store to a `mut` local is refused.
    - Q4.6, Q4.7, Q9.5 and the text-only slice of R4 F4.3 (item 11c): the borrow form is `borrow [mut] view = source ...`.
    - MOD-2, MOD-3, [OD151](#od151) (items 11b, 44): OD151's multi-level paths are written in
      imports, and uses are qualified by the last segment. MOD-2's item-list manifest stands. MOD-3 is revised: two
      same-named items from different modules may both be imported, each used qualified; two
      modules with the same last segment are refused.
    - [OD90](#od90) (item 40): `self` is an ordinary identifier with no special meaning. `Copy`
      stays reserved.
    - [Foundation question 9](#foundation-q9) (item 4): the trap names above.
    - [Review caps](#decided-2026-11-08-review-caps) (items 1, 2): the sign-off path and the cap
      for a diff that mixes classes.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-signoffs"></a>**Owner sign-offs at the review cap** (owner, 2026-11-08:
  "sign off ea5181b5, 1547734f8, a27fbb842, yes redesign"), under [process item 1](#decided-2026-11-08-process). Each sign-off approves one exact SHA
  that Codex, Kimi and agy all reviewed. It never replaces a gate or a proof obligation.
  - `wip/slice0` at `ea5181b535fd2983abfcafe31b3ff7bf59ec6439`: Codex ACCEPT (verification), Kimi
    ACCEPT, agy ACCEPT after withdrawing its findings; branch CI run 42451061873, 18 of 18 jobs green.
  - `wip/record-language` at `1547734f86e83d381f6cac2f25d2810b083bc9ed`: Codex and Kimi ACCEPT
    (verification of the round-2 fixes), agy ACCEPT.
  - `wip/one-repository` at `a27fbb842`: all three ACCEPT, at the scripts cap of 3 rounds.
  None of the three has an outstanding finding.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-landing-redesign"></a>**The landing protection redesign** (owner, 2026-11-08,
  the same answer: "yes redesign"). The local remote guard ("guard A") and the acceptance shim are
  replaced by this order:
  1. the [landing scripts shrink](#decided-2026-11-08-landing-shrink) (R-0617: the bot, notify,
     install-bot and the systemd units go);
  2. then one repository, `lambdaclass/concrete` only (`wip/one-repository`), lands before the
     foundation freeze;
  3. then server-side protection of `main` becomes the real boundary: GitHub rulesets or branch
     protection let only a train merge with the required checks green update `main` (R-0628);
  4. then a much smaller local guard, as a convenience only (R-0629).
  `wip/remote-guard` (`b60670804`) and `wip/acceptance-shim` (`eca268078`) are superseded and do not
  land.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-commit-push"></a>**Commit and push everything all the time** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "commit and push everything all the time"). Every piece is committed as soon as it
  builds and its checks pass, then pushed at once to the push remotes in force. Today those are
  `origin` and `lambdaclass`. Once one repository lands, only `lambdaclass/concrete` remains
  ([landing redesign](#decided-2026-11-08-landing-redesign), step 2). Pushes use `wip/` branches,
  from a full clone, with the hook on, and are never force-pushed. `main` changes only through the train.
  A candidate under review stays fixed; new work goes in new commits or branches.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-writers"></a>**Codex, Kimi and agy write; Claude reviews** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "can you have kimi, codex and agy implement things and we just review it"). Codex, Kimi
  and agy implement. Claude briefs, reviews, runs the gates and lands.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-gates"></a>**Four test gates, growing with the slices** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "do this with kimi, codex and agy and review it" and "we should have ci or gates for
  scaling, determinism, comparison in the spec and the compiler"; then, about the comparison's
  back-end-first shape, "ok wuith this but we have to add it to the roadmap or a slice"; and "ok
  let's make sure the roadmap has all this and the slices").
  1. A spec-vs-compiler comparison uses a seeded generator that grows with each slice (R-0632). It
     ships now at the back-end level: spec semantics versus the LLVM binary versus the proven-path
     binary and its evaluator. It leaves a fixed hook for the full path. The branch that adds
     lowering must also switch on the full spec comparison.
  2. One test manifest and one runner hold the tests (R-0633).
  3. Scaling checks run at 1x, 10x and 100x, with super-linear growth failing; two compiles must be
     byte-identical (R-0634).
  4. A maintained list names the trusted base (R-0635).
  Every slice extends the generator and the manifest and states its trusted-base additions; scaling
  and determinism stay green. This answer covered items 2–5 of the proposal; item 1, showcase
  sketches before the freeze, was not included.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-six"></a>**Six spec and process improvements** (owner, 2026-11-08,
  about 14:00 UTC: "yes to 6 things"):
  1. Every spec law has a non-trivial instance and a planted counter-example that must fail;
     reviewers check each law for vacuity, and already accepted contracts are audited (R-0636).
  2. The axioms golden becomes a property check that permits only the standard axioms, with only
