ROADMAP.md:505:| R-0644 | Phase B1: the slice-1 brief on `wip/slice1-brief`, a draft proposal; nothing launches from it until the owner answers S1-scope, S1-move, S1-abort and S1-toml; owner: `wip/slice1-brief` | the first work is `abort()` in the slice-1 spec, without depending on spelling; then come the slice-1 Core→BIR lowering passes; exit meets the slice shape, including the full spec-vs-native comparison (R-0632) over generated and manifest programs; Home: spec (slice 1 and its lowering) and the proposal `docs/proposals/SLICE1_BRIEF.md`. Gate: launch without the four owner answers is refused, and a planted full-path spec/binary mismatch fails | B0, R-0632, R-0633, owner: S1-scope, S1-move, S1-abort and S1-toml |
ROADMAP.md:506:| R-0425 | The conformance format and suite: each example names its rule, source and outcome (result, refusal code or trap), and one runner per implementation runs it through the Lean semantics and through the compiler | slice 1's examples pass both runners; the suite is versioned and published at 0.1, and complete at D; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0560 |
ROADMAP.md:507:| R-0499 | Independent Core judgments and an authoritative checker proved to correspond | correspondence proved for slice 1, with positive and rule-breaking mutation controls; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0500 |
ROADMAP.md:509:| R-0493 | Typed arithmetic and casts from one semantics, with arithmetic-site facts | widths, signedness, modes and the defined trap match the Lean semantics; first applicable increment at B0; the approved slice-1 obligations remain due at B1 | R-0496 |
ROADMAP.md:511:| R-0501 | Proved lowering passes for sequencing, matching, cleanup and check expansion | each pass preserves behaviour and re-establishes invariants; the slice-0 instances are delivered at B0 and extended cumulatively at B1; every used lowering has preservation and output-invariant proofs | R-0514 |
ROADMAP.md:512:| R-0503 | The unverified BIR → LLVM path, the native driver and the pinned LLVM toolchain | output labelled unverified; LLVM, linker and runtime assumptions listed in their own inventory, separate from the LCB premises; the driver bounds external tools; the slice-0 instance is delivered at B0 and extended cumulatively at B1, labelled unverified; it covers the debug bundle and one build record at B0/B1 (R-0646; revised 2026-11-08, [sweep](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-gates)) | R-0501, R-0581 |
ROADMAP.md:513:| R-0581 | BIR as a complete backend contract shared by LLVM and the LCB ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-proof-infrastructure), 2026-11-06) | BIR's Lean definition fixes integer semantics, layout and endianness, alignment, zero-sized values, internal and foreign calling conventions, external events, trap realization (kind and site) and a frame-accounting handoff; no BIR construct, invariant or lowering choice depends on one backend; stack bytes are never derived from BIR slot counts, only from allocated frames in the final artifact; the slice-0 instance is delivered at B0 | R-0496, R-0502 |
ROADMAP.md:515:| R-0583 | The per-pass proof-or-validator table | each pass is classified as proved directly (the stable, structural ones: parsing, sequencing, matching, cleanup, representation lowering, BIR → LCB-IR) or checked by a proved validator (the search-heavy or changing ones: specialization, SSA construction and destruction, optimizations, register allocation, scheduling, linking), with the reason; the lexer is proved against an independent byte/token judgment; an unclassified pass fails the gate; the slice-0 rows are delivered at B0 | R-0501, R-0504 |
ROADMAP.md:517:| R-0579 | The BIR → LCB-IR adapter refinement proof, the first backend proof target | the adapter's refinement against LCB's imported semantics (not a restatement) is kernel-checked for the slice-0 subset right after the front-end and lowering proofs, at B0, and extended to slice 1 at B1; the theorem is complete: an adapter error produces no artifact, and success implies correctness, so no adapter premise remains ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-adapter)); the end-to-end theorem names the LLVM and LCB premises separately | R-0578, R-0501 |
