ROADMAP.md:529:| R-0620 | The lexer and the LL(1) parser, started now in parallel with the foundation ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-front-end)) | the lexer has comments, whitespace, identifiers, string escapes and decimal literals; it is blocked on the spelling round (R-0625) for the keyword/reserved table, builtin-name table, operator/punctuation set (L1), second escape list (L2), raw control characters in strings (L3), and integer suffixes and base prefixes (L4); the lexer for slice 0's surface runs against the spec's `Lexical` rules as they land (SPEC_UNIFICATION step 8) and does not wait for R-0622, which only the parser and so this task's completion wait for; and the parser is built against the Lean grammar judgment once the owner has approved the written grammar (R-0622), with refusals as diagnostics records (R-0593, which this task feeds rather than waits for); correspondence to the grammar is proved as part of B0; Home: tooling (the compiler's front end), against the spec. Gate: the conformance examples of slice 0's lexical and grammar rules, accepted and refused (revised 2026-11-08, [lexer questions](#open-owner-questions)) | R-0622 |
ROADMAP.md:530:| R-0593 | The diagnostic and facts records, their frozen, versioned JSON schema, goldens and the parser's diagnostics from the first front-end output ([diagnostics decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-project-diagnostics-2026-11-06), [early decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-early-evidence)) | every slice-0 refusal is one typed record rendered as human text and JSON lines (`--format=human|json`), with rule IDs from the Lean rule name and that rule's own revision (R-0603); the diagnostics and facts JSON schema is frozen and versioned from the first front-end output, and golden tests cover it and both diagnostic renderings; the LL(1) parser fills its expected-token set from the grammar data; B0's classified refusal controls use the records; Home: spec (the diagnostics and facts types are the schema source). Gate: the JSON schema generated from the Lean types, with goldens ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0592, R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:532:| R-0585 | Vacuity checks and contract-strength labels from the first proof slice | no fact reads "proved" when its theorem holds only under an unsatisfiable precondition or premise set, from the first proof slice on; the checker reports a context as infeasible wherever its octagon is empty, and a discharge inside an infeasible context is marked vacuous, never established, unless the infeasibility itself is the claim (a dead branch), applying OD120 from B1 rather than F; each proved claim carries its strength (a point instance, one direction, an equivalence or a full contract), so a single-input proof never reads as a universal contract; planted vacuous and point-only proofs are caught; obligation sufficiency stays with R-0460 and the phase-F validation project with R-0219 | R-0575 |
ROADMAP.md:533:| R-0599 | Replayed counterexamples for "not established", without SMT | a deterministic bounded search evaluates a failed obligation's path constraints on boundary points (type extremes and the octagon's constants) and replays a candidate through the Lean reference evaluator with the exact semantics, inputs, resources and environment, satisfying the preconditions; a replayed witness reports "refuted, replayed", otherwise "no witness within N cases", never "safe"; the search never affects admission; due at B1 | R-0575, R-0586 |
ROADMAP.md:534:| R-0600 | A derived "always aborts" fact (SPARK `No_Return`) | a function whose every path aborts gets a derived fact, with no annotation and no function category (OD116); the facts report shows it, and the checker uses it to prune paths after a call to an abort wrapper within the decided divergence typing (TYPE-4@2); any change to the join rule goes to the owner; due with the functions-and-calls slice; the row stays here, and placement (slice 1 or the functions-and-calls slice 5) is an open owner question (R0600 below; revised 2026-11-08, [placement question](#open-owner-questions)) | R-0575, owner: R0600 placement |
ROADMAP.md:536:| R-0586 | The I/O driver for the Lean reference evaluator | the Lean evaluator runs programs that read and write their grants, so I/O examples get expected outcomes independent of the compiler; its infrastructure lands with slice 1 (B1), and from slice 16 (the console) it runs every supported I/O example and produces its expected observations independently of the compiler, and R-0491 uses them | R-0496, R-0425 |
ROADMAP.md:540:| R-0118 | Generated coverage of passes, consumers, constructs, operations and traps | a missing row or a synthetic constructor fails the gate; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:542:| R-0498 | Every enforcement claim maps to its gate leg and a targeted mutation | an unmapped claim fails; shape checks are labelled unproved; B0 includes pipeline-edge coverage and controls for missing proofs, unregistered premises, wrong artifact substitution and reject-all compilation | R-0118 |
ROADMAP.md:545:| R-0630 | Phase B0: gate 2 for `Concrete/`: axioms and trust over the compiler; owner: the slice-0 worker | at slice 0 (B0), over every constant of `Concrete/`: only the three axioms, no `sorry`, no `native_decide`/`ofReduceBool`, the totality ban (no `partial`, `unsafe`, `@[implemented_by]`, `@[extern]` or `opaque` outside `Premises.lean`) and the external-checker replay; today only the trusted-base golden covers the headline theorems' axioms (slice-0 worker, 2026-11-08); Home: tooling (CI). Gate: the job itself, with a planted `sorry`, a planted `partial` and a planted axiom refused ([tracking](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-tracking)) | R-0553 |
ROADMAP.md:546:| R-0553 | Totality audit: every semantics, checker, validator and pass is total and runs as proved | no partial definition; opaque execution gaps controlled; totality and execution-gap checks apply from B0; B3 performs the cumulative audit after B2 | R-0501 |
ROADMAP.md:549:| R-0569 | A new website, built from scratch with the new compiler (decision row P9) | the site is designed anew around the decided language; its playground runs the new compiler; it teaches and links the Lean rules and states no rule of its own; no page describes the legacy compiler or the language before the 2026-11 decisions as current | B1 |
ROADMAP.md:555:| R-0572 | The LCB pilot (LP0): a BIR → LCB → native proof on the subset of the slices before LP0, before substantial feature growth (decided 2026-11-06, revised the same day: the LCB joins slice 0) | the subset, already built unverified through the LCB since slice 0 (R-0578), carries a native proof: the adapter proof (R-0579) composed with the LCB's theorems for its exact domain and with the slice theorem under named premises, kept separate from the LLVM premises; every remaining backend obligation has its own task and milestone; native proof closure for the profile (R-0517) follows | B1, R-0579 |
ROADMAP.md:630:| R-0201 | Audit commands over the slice-0 facts query (R-0575): `audit`, `explain`, `why`, `diff` and trust-diff gates | the package-scale audit bundle passes; the per-function query itself is R-0575 (B0/B1), not this task | R-0178 |
ROADMAP.md:652:| R-0646 | The CLI question audit's placements; owner: `wip/cli-question-audit` @ `23d929403` | A2i/C9, before the facts schema freezes: U1 obligation identity; U2 edit-stable keys; U3 package identity; U4 specialization identity and request edges; U7 one span type; U12 typed premise identity. B0: U5 stage-named refusals and a rule for failures after admission; U8 IR text printers; U11 generating-rule origins; U13 every BIR instruction's origin. U6 front-end recovery is the B0/B1 owner question below. R-0503 covers the debug bundle and one build record at B0/B1. Slice 8: U9 obligations exported as Lean propositions; U10 proof subject binding. Views land with their constructs: `why` stays R-0201 over slice-5 call evidence; trust-edge and proof-dependency views at 5, the latter extended at 8; layout at 9; generic-instance at 11; traceability at B0 and through Mono at 11; fact-kind selection with the slice-0 facts. Home: spec (C9 identities) and tooling (the front end, BIR, printers, facts views). Gate: each placed item gets its slice's conformance or golden control, and an urgent item U1–U13 without a placement fails the audit | — |
ROADMAP.md:664:| R-0617 | Smaller landing scripts ([decision](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-landing-shrink)) | `scripts/land/` keeps the train, its review leg, the gates and the `legacy/` protection; the landing bot, notify, install-bot, the systemd units and duplicate helpers are removed and their old copies moved to `legacy/` as byte-identical additions, the one kind of addition `legacy_frozen` accepts once legacy sweep question 1 ("ok to 1", [sweep row](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-legacy-sweep)) lands; Home: tooling. Gate: the root checks (`landing_script`, `legacy_frozen`, the root inventory) pass without them | — |
docs/decisions.md:15:The shape of Core was approved provisionally on 2026-11-05 ([stage A2a](#decided-2026-11-05)): it binds the design, and the owner freezes Core at stage A2c, after the four design theorems are proved against the proposed `Spec.lean` and the integration stage A2i (2026-11-06) is accepted. The project and architecture decisions are recorded here too, [below](#decided-project).
docs/decisions.md:56:- The slice-0 surface: `concrete build`, `concrete facts`, unsupported-form refusals, leading zeros ([row](#decided-slice0-surface-2026-11-06)).
docs/decisions.md:188:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
docs/decisions.md:190:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
docs/decisions.md:195:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
docs/decisions.md:200:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped. *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): the example's unlabeled pattern (`S { a }`) and unannotated local are superseded by items 9 and 18; the typed replacement comes in the spelling round (R-0625).*
docs/decisions.md:254:- **Q6.4** Does unreachable code receive static refusal checks? — Check all written code, including generic bodies. *Revises or adds: REVISES TYPE-22@1 examples.*
docs/decisions.md:289:- **Q9.14** What must pass before Core freezes? — Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze. Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): the integration stage A2i (interaction proofs, semantic contract, modular layout, owner view, outside-reader pass) also passes before the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:294:- **Q9.19** How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate? — A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze. *Revises or adds: Process: owner decision.* Revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): integration (A2i) comes between A2b and the A2c freeze, and the modular layout (R-0560) precedes the freeze.
docs/decisions.md:321:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
