  None of the three has an outstanding finding.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-landing-redesign"></a>**The landing protection redesign** (owner, 2026-11-08,
  the same answer: "yes redesign"). The local remote guard ("guard A") and the acceptance shim are
  replaced by this order:
  1. the [landing scripts shrink](#decided-2026-11-08-landing-shrink) (R-0617: the bot, notify,
     install-bot and the systemd units go);
  2. then one repository, `lambdaclass/concrete` only (`wip/one-repository`), lands before the
     foundation freeze;
  3. then server-side protection of `main` becomes the real boundary: GitHub rulesets or branch
     protection let only a train merge with the required checks green update `main` (R-0628);
  4. then a much smaller local guard, as a convenience only (R-0629).
  `wip/remote-guard` (`b60670804`) and `wip/acceptance-shim` (`eca268078`) are superseded and do not
  land.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-commit-push"></a>**Commit and push everything all the time** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "commit and push everything all the time"). Every piece is committed as soon as it
  builds and its checks pass, then pushed at once to the push remotes in force. Today those are
  `origin` and `lambdaclass`. Once one repository lands, only `lambdaclass/concrete` remains
  ([landing redesign](#decided-2026-11-08-landing-redesign), step 2). Pushes use `wip/` branches,
  from a full clone, with the hook on, and are never force-pushed. `main` changes only through the train.
  A candidate under review stays fixed; new work goes in new commits or branches.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-writers"></a>**Codex, Kimi and agy write; Claude reviews** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "can you have kimi, codex and agy implement things and we just review it"). Codex, Kimi
  and agy implement. Claude briefs, reviews, runs the gates and lands.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-gates"></a>**Four test gates, growing with the slices** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "do this with kimi, codex and agy and review it" and "we should have ci or gates for
  scaling, determinism, comparison in the spec and the compiler"; then, about the comparison's
  back-end-first shape, "ok wuith this but we have to add it to the roadmap or a slice"; and "ok
  let's make sure the roadmap has all this and the slices").
  1. A spec-vs-compiler comparison uses a seeded generator that grows with each slice (R-0632). It
     ships now at the back-end level: spec semantics versus the LLVM binary versus the proven-path
     binary and its evaluator. It leaves a fixed hook for the full path. The branch that adds
     lowering must also switch on the full spec comparison.
  2. One test manifest and one runner hold the tests (R-0633).
  3. Scaling checks run at 1x, 10x and 100x, with super-linear growth failing; two compiles must be
     byte-identical (R-0634).
  4. A maintained list names the trusted base (R-0635).
  Every slice extends the generator and the manifest and states its trusted-base additions; scaling
  and determinism stay green. This answer covered items 2–5 of the proposal; item 1, showcase
  sketches before the freeze, was not included.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-six"></a>**Six spec and process improvements** (owner, 2026-11-08,
  about 14:00 UTC: "yes to 6 things"):
  1. Every spec law has a non-trivial instance and a planted counter-example that must fail;
     reviewers check each law for vacuity, and already accepted contracts are audited (R-0636).
  2. The axioms golden becomes a property check that permits only the standard axioms, with only
     named theorems pinned (R-0638).
  3. Accepted foundation contracts merge into `wip/spec-core` continuously, starting at A2i (R-0637).
  4. Process tooling is frozen once owner-signoff, its split follow-up and landing-shrink (R-0617)
     land. Independent branches land independently instead of being chained (R-0640).
  5. One shared launcher pairs every background job with a bounded watcher (R-0639).
  6. Every owner question gets a Codex, Kimi and agy round before it reaches the owner (R-0641).
  The decision records these outcomes, not the process for implementing them.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-legacy-sweep"></a>**Byte-for-byte sweeps into `legacy/`** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "ok to 1", answering legacy sweep question 1). `legacy_frozen.sh` may accept an addition
  under `legacy/` only if it is byte-identical to the same root path at a named commit, and the file
  is gone from the root. Every other addition or edit stays refused, and the rule has negative
  controls. The sweeps are recorded in `scripts/checks/legacy.lock`. The check judges the history
  that landed on `main`: no lock line is rewritten or removed, and no `legacy/` file changes from a
  which all four reviewing models recommended keeping today's design, because `with(...)` is the
  checked ceiling in a function's signature when structs own capabilities. Follow-up for the
  allocation slice: whether destroying a collection still needs `with(Alloc)` when its release
  metadata travels with it (R-0624, raised by agy).
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-language"></a>**Language and process decisions of 2026-11-08** (owner,
  2026-11-08: "record, yes to all recommended", approving the list as recommended; "i prefer fully
  gone, self and as are irrelevant from what i see", on `self` and `as`; and "i like 3", on how
  imported items are used). Codex, Kimi and agy reviewed the list in six rounds and agreed on every
  item except 11(b), which the owner then decided by choosing option 3. The spellings of items 6–29
  and 40–53 are illustrative; their exact spellings come in the [spelling round](#decided-2026-11-08-spelling-round).
  The trap names (item 4) and the grammar items 30–39 are decided as written.
  The numbers are the list's.
  - <a id="decided-2026-11-08-process"></a>*Process.*
    1. *Sign-off when the review cap is reached.* The owner may approve one exact SHA after Codex,
       Kimi and agy have all reviewed it. The approval and every outstanding finding and verdict
       are recorded. It never replaces a gate or a proof obligation.
    2. *A diff that touches several classes* gets the highest of their caps (docs 2, scripts 3,
       compiler and spec 5), classified by the files the diff actually changes. This settles the
       question [review caps](#decided-2026-11-08-review-caps) left to the owner.
    3. *One review round on where each std item lives* ([std per slice](#decided-2026-11-08-std-table)).
    4. *Trap names:* `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `out_of_bounds`,
        `extern`.
    23. *No packed structs* until a real packed foreign ABI needs them. A demonstrated ABI
        reopens the question.
    24. *Local `assume`* is removed only after its replacement, named trusted contracts with
        evidence identity, has been shown on real examples, point-local conditional facts
        included. Until then it stays explicit and tracked, and it never admits a safety
        obligation.
    25. *Function values* keep the decided design: a function pointer plus explicit context, with
        contracts.
    26. *Constants are computed at compile time*, and a constant's value must be `Copy`. Before
        Q6.4 and TYPE-22 are revised, the closed total subset of initializers is defined and the
        decided conditions for reopening compile-time evaluation are checked.
    27. *Kept explicitly:* value-producing `if` and `match`, both proof scopes, and existential
        brands.
    28. *SPARK 4(b):* an optional `#[depends(result <- a, b)]`, checked against the derived fact and
        never trusted.
    29. *SPARK 4(c):* a dead store to a `mut` local is refused, extending [OD62](#od62).
        output and append, and the typing rules for destructuring and ghost bindings, together
        with every spelling from items 6–29 and 40–53. They are shown to the owner as before/after code.
    56. The entropy grant's shape, at slice 6.
    57. The concurrency model, at slice 28.
    58. Niches and smaller enum tags, only if a workload needs them.
    59. The remaining open grammar sub-rules come back to the owner in the spelling round with
        item 55, as before/after code:
        - the numeric token policy (OPEN-5);
        - attribute duplication, ordering, attachment and scope, the struct-invariant scope
          included (OPEN-15);
        - zero-count array repetition (OPEN-20);
        - the closed builtin inventory, and exhaustive signatures for the view, constant-time,
          text, container and branded APIs (OPEN-4/21);

- **Foundation Q1:** May a fallible operation appear in a constant initializer: a closed always-succeeds subset, arithmetic admitted but failing evaluation refused, or admission only with closed evidence of success? Codex and Kimi recommend the closed subset; agy recommends the arithmetic rule, and all three add the closed-evidence condition. Source: `PENDING_RECORDS.md`, spec worker ~06:30 (no branch or SHA recorded).
- **Foundation Q2:** Should forward constant references be order-independent, with cycles refused and the dependency path reported, or should forward references be refused? All three reviewers recommend order-independent references. Source: `PENDING_RECORDS.md`, spec worker ~06:30 (no branch or SHA recorded).
- **Foundation Q3:** Can a pointer be formed from partial bytes: no pointer meaning, or a separate named bytes-to-address conversion without provenance? All three reviewers recommend no implicit pointer meaning, with the separate conversion as the missing option. Source: `PENDING_RECORDS.md`, spec worker ~06:30 (no branch or SHA recorded).
- **S1:** How should safe displacement be scheduled: (a) place-level `replace` at 13 and two-index view `swap` at 15, (b) `replace` at 13 only, or (c) neither before 24, using a sorted-array map or by-value APIs? Reviewers' proposal recommends (a). Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md` (S1).
- **S2:** May one view lend shared references to two elements at once for comparison: (a) yes, inside a borrow block and never returned or stored, or (b) no, use a callback at 20? Reviewers' proposal recommends (a). Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md` (S2).
- **S3:** What shape should one stream interface take: (a) per-family functions with one shared Lean law, (b) a generic function with explicit capability and function-value arguments at 20, or (c) an associated-capability trait? Reviewers' proposal recommends comparing (a) at 18 and (b) at 20 in D4, with (c) only if both fail. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md` (S3).
- **S4:** What non-consuming traversal callback should exist: (a) reuse `Step` with a borrowing callback, or (b) use only a coordinate cursor? Reviewers' proposal recommends deciding after D3 compares them. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md` (S4).
- **S5:** Which float operations does slice 26 need, and should NaN observations use (a) canonical arithmetic NaNs, (b) Wasm-style allowed-result sets with relational bit observations, or (c) bit-exact backend payloads as a named premise? Reviewers' proposal recommends (a). Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md` (S5).
- **O1:** How is a view parameter spelled: `View<T>` / `ViewMut<T>`, or `&[T]` / `&mut [T]`? The proposal recommends the named constructors. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O1).
- **O2:** How can grant-owned arguments and variables be borrowed: make the launch snapshot a borrow root and return `Copy` coordinates, or copy bytes into a caller buffer? The proposal recommends the borrow root and coordinates. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O2).
- **O3:** Is `Vec` a language container with per-slot initialization state, or std code over a new primitive capacity buffer? The proposal recommends a language container. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O3).
- **O4:** How is a non-`Copy` container element read without returned references: a downward function-value callback, or a scoped element-borrow form? The proposal recommends one callback form. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O4).
- **O5:** Should wide arithmetic use 64-bit limbs, or add `u128` or a widening-multiply primitive after measurements? The proposal recommends limbs now. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O5).
- **O6:** Are view `ct_eq` and UTF-8 validation primitives or std code, how does validation form a text view, and do primitives keep bare builtin names or move to qualified std paths? The proposal recommends std UTF-8 validation with the decided borrow form and bare primitive names. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O6).
- **O7:** Does the closed constant-time table include 64-bit multiplication, with a named backend premise where it is variable-time, and rotations by public counts, or are those operations excluded? Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O7).
- **O8:** Are array literals of literal elements allowed in the closed constant-initializer subset, with computed tables waiting, or are computed arrays included now? The proposal recommends literal elements now and computed tables later. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O8).
- **O9:** Should public and secret data have two plain implementations, or one generic implementation over a new trait? The proposal recommends two implementations until a consumer needs one generic body. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O9).
- **O10:** Does a function return several results in a named struct, or through `&mut` output parameters once references exist? The proposal recommends a named struct. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O10).
- **O11:** Are trait functional laws proved as Lean theorems with explicit premises, or declared in a new trait-level law construct checked at each impl? The proposal recommends Lean theorems and no new language feature. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O11).
- **O12:** How does entropy use its grant: (A) a `Copy` grant with a draw at 7, (B) a linear grant with a mutable-lending draw at 13, or (C) a linear grant with a grant-returning draw at 9? The proposal recommends B for the slice-6 decision. Source: `wip/std-placement` @ `abc648e4d`, `docs/proposals/STD_OWNER_QUESTIONS.md` (O12).
- **L1:** Did item 34 approve the grammar sheet's section-2 token set: (a) yes, closed, (b) only the rule, with inventory left to the spelling round, or (c) approved except tokens touched by items 6, 11, 15 and 49? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (L1).
- **L2:** Which literal owns the second escape list: (a) a single-quoted ASCII `u8` literal, (b) no single-quoted literal and one escape list, or (c) a byte-string literal with `\xHH`? Kimi and agy recommend (a); Codex recommends (b). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (L2).
- **L3:** Are raw control characters other than LF and CR allowed in strings: (a) all refused, (b) raw tab allowed, or (c) all allowed? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (L3).
- **L4:** What integer suffix and base grammar applies: (a) eight type-name suffixes after any base, lowercase `0x`/`0b`/`0o`, no underscore or float suffix, (b) (a) without octal, (c) decimal suffixes only, or (d) no prefixes? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (L4).
- **S1-scope:** Should slice 1 use (a) a compiler-admitted subset of a wider slice-1 spec, with subset predicates and preservation proofs, or (b) a spec narrowed to the compiler slice? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (S1-scope); draft brief: `wip/slice1-brief` @ `28b42ade9`, `docs/proposals/SLICE1_BRIEF.md`.
- **S1-move:** Does consuming a linear value use (a) explicit `move t`, or (b) plain `t` elaborated to Core `move t`? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (S1-move).
- **S1-abort:** Where may `abort()` appear in slice 1: (a) only `return abort();` with bottom typing, (b) a let initializer or return value, (c) brought-forward `abort();` statements, or (d) another form? Codex, Kimi and agy recommend (a). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (S1-abort).
- **S1-toml:** Should `Concrete.toml` use (a) top-level `version = 1`, `[policy] predictable = true` and the five `[assumptions]` kinds, (b) per-table versions and `[policy] profile = "predictable"`, or (c) another shape? Codex and Kimi recommend (b); agy recommends (a); all three ask for an explicit trusted-assumptions setting. Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (S1-toml).
- **R0600:** Is the derived always-aborts fact placed at (a) slice 1, (b) slice 5 with removal from B1, or (c) split between a trivial `main` fact at 1 and call pruning at 5? Kimi and agy recommend (b); Codex recommends (c). Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (R0600).
- **F-NaN:** Should NaN semantics use (a) canonical NaNs, (b) hardware/IEEE payload propagation, (c) no NaN-producing operation without proof or a checked form, or (d) (c) plus canonical NaNs for admitted NaN? Codex and Kimi recommend scoped (d): proof or checked creation, canonical arithmetic results only, and bit-exact representation and sign operations; agy recommends (c) with finite-by-invariant floats. For float equality, Codex and Kimi recommend IEEE `==` and agy a NaN-free reflexive rule; all recommend separately named bit equality. Source: `wip/owner-questions-review` @ `11d9e6ee2`, `docs/proposals/OWNER_QUESTIONS_REVIEW.md` (F-NaN and float `==`).
