### Language and process decisions (2026-11-08)

`docs/decisions.md` records the owner's approval of the 2026-11-08 list ("record, yes to all
recommended"), the answer on `self` and `as` ("i prefer fully gone, self and as are irrelevant from
what i see") and the choice for imported items ("i like 3"). Each revised row carries an explicit
revision note.

- **Process:** the owner may sign off one exact SHA at the review cap after Codex, Kimi and agy
  reviewed it. A diff that mixes classes takes the highest cap. The trap names are `overflow`,
  `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `out_of_bounds`, `invalid_range` and `abort`.
- **Language:**
  - no method calls or receivers: calls are `Type::f(x)` or `Trait::f(x)`, with a typed first
    parameter;
  - constructors and patterns always write `field: value` and `field: binder`;
  - `as` is removed entirely and is not reserved; casts become one named lossless conversion;
  - imports are item lists, and every imported item is used qualified by its module's last
    segment, never bare;
  - a named heap move-out replaces `*h`;
  - no `char` type and no `print`, `println` or `append` builtins;
  - every local and function result has a written type;
  - `OrderedMap` only for now, and no packed structs;
  - handwritten bodyless functions are refused;
  - constants are compile-time and `Copy`;
  - `#[depends(...)]` is checked, and dead stores to `mut` locals are refused;
  - `assume` stays until its replacement is shown on real examples;
  - the grammar choices of the grammar review are recorded.
- **Later:** the spelling round shows every exact name and spelling to the owner as before/after
  code. It comes before the Lean grammar.
- **ROADMAP:** R-0625 (the spelling round, before R-0622), R-0626 (the `Process` foreign-declaration
  audit) and R-0627 (the `assume` replacement, shown before removal) are added. The foundation rows
  gain the trap names, the typed first parameter, the mathematical-arithmetic marker, compile-time
  constants, dead-store refusal and the `#[depends]` check. The slice table follows the decisions;
  the std introduction leaves the placement revisions to the separate std placement round; and R-0618 takes the mixed-class cap and the sign-off path.

exec
/bin/bash -lc "cat > /tmp/record_edit.py <<'PY'
from pathlib import Path
D=Path('docs/decisions.md'); R=Path('ROADMAP.md'); A=Path('AGENTS.md')
d=D.read_text(); r=R.read_text(); a=A.read_text()
link='#decided-2026-11-08-twenty-nine'
note=' *Revised 2026-11-08 by the [29 decisions]('+link+').*'
rnote=' Revised 2026-11-08 by the [29 decisions](docs/decisions.md'+link+').'
gnote=' Revised 2026-11-08 by the [gate tiers](docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-08-gate-tiers).'
# Preserve the supplied final wording, grouped and numbered, without scratch paths.
source=Path('.fix-inputs/OWNER_29_DECISIONS.md').read_text()
body=source[source.index('## A. Process'):source.index('Not covered by this answer:')]
import re
body=re.sub(r' REVISES["'^'"\\n]*','',body)
body='\\n'.join('  '+x if x else '' for x in body.splitlines()).replace('  ## ','  **').replace('. Process\\n','. Process**\\n')
# Use real group headings; the item numbering remains the input numbering.
body=re.sub(r'  \\*\\*(["'^'"\\n]+)(?<"'!'"\\*)\\n',r'  **\\1**\\n',body)
body=body.replace('**A. Process****','**A. Process**')
new='''- <a id=\"decided-2026-11-08-twenty-nine\"></a>**The 29 owner decisions** (owner, 2026-11-08, 15:08 UTC): \"ok but i said already all yes based on the best long term reviews of each agent and what we said before right?\" All 29 final picks are decided, including the second rounds on items 3, 14 and 18. The wording below records those picks. Exact spellings otherwise remain subject to R-0625.
'''+body+'''
  Operating rule from ISS-0021: every reviewer, agy included, runs where it can read the repository and names the files it read. On record-foundation, Codex wrote the round-2 fixes and the Codex leg ran; that writer/reviewer overlap is recorded. R-0650 fixes the tooling. The owner sign-offs of legacy sweep "'`35b489e4` and B `e997548d` are recorded once that work lands; the former is the legacy sweep, not the roadmap sweep.
  Item 30 was answered separately at 15:11:33 UTC: "sign off e997548d i amn gonna grab a coffee, gonna come back in an hour". This approves only `e997548d93482f37181dc0a523a2a06bca40a77c` on `wip/one-repository-2`: Codex ACCEPT (fix check, full access), agy ACCEPT; Kimi wrote it and did not review. The three C5 questions and the gate-tier assignment remain open.
- <a id="decided-2026-11-08-long-term-reviews"></a>**Best long-term design** (owner, 2026-11-08): "ALWAYS tell the reviewrs to do the best long term thing rather than cuitting corners". Every writer and reviewer prompt to Codex, Kimi or agy explicitly says to judge and fix by the best long-term design, never cutting corners for speed, a smaller diff or fewer rounds. Reviewers report shortcuts as real findings and name the long-term fix. Writers implement the structural fix. If staging is unavoidable, they record the long-term design and its roadmap row.
'"'''
d=d.replace('<a id=\"rules-a-decision-changed\"></a>',new+'\\n<a id=\"rules-a-decision-changed\"></a>',1)
d=d.replace('- The core statement', '- The 29 final picks on process, lexer, slice 1, floats, CLI, foundation, std, sign-offs and planning ([row]('+link+')).\\n- Best long-term writer and reviewer design ([row](#decided-2026-11-08-long-term-reviews)).\\n- Approved gate additions and three tiers; assignments remain open ([row](#decided-2026-11-08-gate-tiers)).\\n- The core statement',1)
# Gate content, each approved item retained as a short phrase from its proposal.
g=Path('.fix-inputs/GATE_PROPOSALS.md').read_text()
improvements=g[g.index('### C,'):g.index('## Proposed tiers')]
improvements=improvements.replace('### C, scaling and determinism','**C/D: scaling and determinism**').replace('### A, spec vs compiler','**A: spec vs compiler**').replace('### Trusted list','**Trusted list**').replace('### All gates','**All gates**').replace('## New gates 1-12','**New gates 1–12**')
