ROADMAP.md:467:| R-0647 | Phase B0: all-gates improvements G1–G3; owner: the gates worker | G1. A meta-gate over the gate manifest. Every gate declares its property, controls, cost, location and tier, and has at least one planted control that fails.; G2. A disagreement between reruns is a failure, never a retry-pass.; G3. Track each gate's cost. Home: tooling. Gate: manifest controls reject missing metadata/controls, rerun disagreement and missing cost records. | R-0649 |
ROADMAP.md:468:| R-0648 | Phase B0: new gates 1–12; owner: the gates worker | 1. Rule-ID refusal: every refusal names a spec rule, and every rule has a refusal fixture. Gate: rule/refusal coverage. 2. Mechanized vacuity: every law has an instance and a planted counter-example. Gate: law instance and failing counter-example (R-0636). 3. Decision-to-spec traceability, in both directions. Gate: bidirectional decision/spec links. 4. Lexer and parser fuzzing: no crash, termination, refusal with a rule ID, and the round-trip parse(print(ast)) = ast. Gate: parser fuzz and round-trip controls. 5. Backend agreement: LLVM and LCB agree on the whole manifest. Gate: whole-manifest LLVM/LCB observations. 6. Trap-site accounting: every emitted trap maps to an obligation and a trap name. Gate: trap/obligation/name mapping. 7. Output binary audit: symbols, sections and imports. Gate: symbols/sections/imports audit. 8. Toolchain and supply chain: pinned and hashed Lean, LLVM and the nix lock, reproduced by a clean rebuild. Gate: pin/hash clean rebuild. 9. Documentation examples compile or refuse exactly as stated. Gate: example result agreement. 10. A proof-check time budget per theorem, with history. Gate: per-theorem time budget/history. 11. Import layering: compiler code must not import test or spec-diff code. Gate: forbidden-import control. 12. Gate coverage: every pipeline stage is covered by at least one gate. Gate: stage/gate coverage control. Home: tooling. | R-0647, R-0636 |
ROADMAP.md:469:| R-0649 | Phase B0: PR, daily and weekly gate tiers; owner: the gates worker | OPEN: assign each gate to the PR, daily or weekly tier in the three-model round. Every gate declares its tier, and the meta-gate enforces this along with the PR-tier budget. A daily or weekly failure is a stop-the-line bug against main, at the exact SHA, with a reproducer. It never blocks an unrelated landing, and it is never ignored. Every bug a scheduled run finds adds a regression fixture to the PR tier. A skipped or cancelled scheduled run counts as missing, never as passed. Home: tooling. Gate: meta-gate checks declarations and PR budget; controls reject ignored scheduled failures, missing regressions and skipped/cancelled runs reported as passed. | R-0647 |
ROADMAP.md:470:| R-0652 | Phase B0: test types T-a–T-j; owner: the gates worker | T-a metamorphic: rename, reorder, dead code, reformat. Gate: transformation agreement. T-b exhaustive arithmetic over 8-bit operands, with boundary values at wider widths. Gate: all 8-bit operands and wider boundaries. T-c a validator after every pass. Gate: invalid-pass-output control. T-d valgrind on emitted binaries. Gate: valgrind failures. T-e C layout and ABI comparison with clang. Gate: clang layout/ABI comparison. T-f golden diagnostics. Gate: diagnostic goldens. T-g theorem-statement pinning. Gate: changed-statement control. T-h limit tests. Gate: boundary/over-limit controls. T-i termination and bounded-execution refusals. Gate: termination/budget refusal controls. T-j a regression fixture for every bug. Gate: bug/fixture coverage audit. Tier assignment remains R-0649. Home: tooling. | R-0649 |
