docs/decisions.md:1773:| OD42 | <a id="od42"></a>Comparisons on `bool` and `char` under OD26 (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26, whose answer says "arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only"). Two readings: numbers only, so `b == true` and `c < 'z'` are refused (a `bool` is tested directly, a `char` converted by a named function); or "numbers" excludes pointers only, and `bool` and `char` keep equality (and `char` ordering). Recommendation: keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, and refuse only pointers, which is what OD26's alignment with TRUST-1 is about Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, refuse ordering on `bool`; amend TYPE-6 and OD26 explicitly | the current compiler compares `bool` and `char` | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `==`/`!=` on bool/char and `<` on char; refuse ordering on bool **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there is no `char`; `==` and `!=` stay on `bool`, and character comparisons become explicit `u8` and UTF-8 operations.** |
docs/decisions.md:1837:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
docs/decisions.md:1838:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
docs/decisions.md:1868:| OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII **Revised 2026-11-08 by the [language decisions](#decided-2026-11-08-language-revisions): there is no `char` type. Revised 2026-11-08 by [item 3](#decided-2026-11-08-twenty-nine): ASCII quoted literals have type u8; non-ASCII is refused.** |
docs/decisions.md:2132:- <a id="OPEN-107"></a>**OPEN-107** (OD107, What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values): See [OD107](#od107).

